    "key": "C",
    "explanation": "Ibadat Khana ('House of Worship') was a hall built by Akbar in 1575 at Fatehpur Sikri specifically for religious discussions and debates. Akbar invited scholars from different religions — Islam, Hinduism, Christianity, Jainism, Zoroastrianism — to discuss and debate their faiths. This exposure eventually led Akbar to create 'Din-i-Ilahi' (a syncretic faith).\r\n\r\n**Key distinction:**\r\n\r\n(a) It was NOT a mosque.\r\n\r\n(b) It was NOT Akbar's private prayer room.\r\n\r\n(d) It was NOT just for nobles — it was for scholars of various religions invited by Akbar.\r\n\r\n(Ref: NCERT Class 7, Our Pasts-II, p.54)"
