    "year": 2014,
    "subject": "History",
    "format": "single",
    "stem": "With reference to the cultural history of India, the term 'Panchayatan' refers to",
    "options": [
      "an assembly of village elders",
      "a religious sect",
      "a style of temple construction",
      "an administrative functionary"
    ],
    "key": "C",
    "explanation": "Panchayatana is a style of Hindu temple construction where one main shrine (dedicated to the principal deity) is surrounded by four smaller subsidiary shrines at the four corners. The word literally means 'group of five.' It's like a quincunx pattern (one in the centre, four at corners). This style is found in many North Indian Nagara-style temples. Famous examples: Kandariya Mahadeva Temple (Khajuraho), Lakshmana Temple (Khajuraho), Dashavatara Temple (Deogarh).\r\n\r\n**Don't confuse with:**\r\n\r\n(a) Panchayat (village council),\r\n\r\n(b) any religious sect, or\r\n\r\n(d) any administrative post. The '-tan' suffix relates to a structural arrangement, not governance.\r\n\r\n(Ref: NCERT + CCRT website)"
