    "subject": "Environment",
    "format": "single",
    "stem": "If a wetland of international importance is brought under the 'Montreux Record', what does it imply ?",
    "options": [
      "Changes in ecological character have occurred, are occurring or are likely to occur in the wetland as a result of human interference.",
      "The country in which the wetland is located should enact a low to prohibit any human activity within five kilometres from the edge of the wetland.",
      "The survival of the wetland depends on the cultural practices and traditions of certain communities living in its vicinity and therefore the cultural diversity therein should not be destroyed.",
      "It is given the status of 'World Heritage Site'."
    ],
    "key": "A",
    "explanation": "The Montreux Record is a register of Ramsar wetland sites where ecological changes have occurred, are occurring, or are likely to occur due to human interference. Think of it as a 'red flag list' or 'danger list' for wetlands. Being on the Montreux Record means the wetland needs urgent attention and conservation measures. It does NOT mean:\r\n\r\n(b) A 5 km no-activity zone is created — no such rule exists.\r\n\r\n(c) Cultural diversity protection — this confuses it with UNESCO cultural heritage concepts.\r\n\r\n(d) World Heritage Site status — that's a separate UNESCO designation. India's Montreux Record sites have included Chilika Lake (removed after improvement), Loktak Lake, and Keoladeo Ghana NP."
